  "content": {
    "id": "p19-q33-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "The sum of six positive integers is 80. What is the smallest possible value of their LCM?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$15$.\n\nThe integers $15,15,15,15,15,5$ have sum $80$ and LCM $15$; smaller LCMs cannot give the required sum.",
      "worked": "Let $L$ be their LCM. Each integer divides $L$, so each is at most $L$. If $L\\le13$, their sum is at most $6\\times13=78<80$.\n\nIf $L=14$, each integer belongs to $\\{1,2,7,14\\}$. Six copies of $14$ sum to $84$. Replacing any copy with a smaller divisor reduces the sum by at least $7$, so a sum of $80$ is impossible.\n\nFor $L=15$, choose $15,15,15,15,15,5$:\n$$\\begin{align*}15+15+15+15+15+5&=80,\\\\\\operatorname{LCM}(15,15,15,15,15,5)&=15.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, the smallest possible LCM is $15$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 76
  },
